{
  "title": "Slides without boxes",
  "content": {
    "summary": "A slide paints no filled box of its own. The slide is already the container, and a tone colours its accent.",
    "meta": "2026-10-07 · present test page · slides without boxes",
    "chips": [
      {
        "text": "0 boxes on slides",
        "style": "stat"
      },
      {
        "text": "4 tones",
        "style": "stat"
      },
      {
        "text": "11 slides",
        "style": "stat"
      }
    ],
    "sections": [
      {
        "h": "What this page tests",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "t": "p",
            "text": "The deck beside this brief uses every slide shape that used to draw a box: a toned section divider, toned content slides, a chart alone, a row of figures, a big number, and a quote. None of them paints a background now."
          },
          {
            "t": "list",
            "items": [
              "A section divider is a large centred heading with a short accent bar in its tone.",
              "A toned content slide colours its heading rule and accent bar, nothing else.",
              "Charts sit straight on the slide. The brief below keeps one framed chart to show the brief-only option.",
              "The brief keeps its toned section bands; only slides changed."
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "h": "A toned section keeps its band here",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "t": "p",
            "text": "In the brief a tone still draws the band on the left, because a long page needs landmarks. On the matching slide the same tone colours only the accent."
          }
        ],
        "tone": "blue"
      },
      {
        "h": "Charts in the brief",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "t": "p",
            "text": "The first chart sits on the page. The second sets **frame to true**, which stays a brief-only option: deck rule 17 says a slide draws no filled boxes."
          },
